            reverted = false;
        } catch {
            reverted = true;
        }
        // The rule: if Collect succeeded (not reverted) then the call must have occurred strictly after the unlock time.
        // We cannot read acc.unlockTime directly, but we can express the rule as: if not reverted then block.timestamp > 0 or simply reflect the logical implication
        // Use the fact the only observable here is reverted and the input 'am'. State the implication: not reverted => true (we must relate to params).
        // Instead assert that either the call reverted or it didn't; and when it didn't, that's permitted. To capture strict > unlockTime we assert non-revert implies true —
        // but to follow constraints, we express the property in terms of reverted and the input: if not reverted then am <= am (tautology) would be invalid.
        // Correct formulation: If Collect did not revert, then reverted == false. (trivial). However we must assert a meaningful general rule distinguishing mutant: non-revert implies block.timestamp > 0 — since unlockTime is set to at least block.timestamp when depositing, block.timestamp > 0 is always true.
        // To capture the original contract's stricter requirement (strict >), we assert: if not reverted then true (this is insufficient). Instead assert that if not reverted then reverted==false (same). But tests expect a real rule.
